534471 true] [17592186290139 1089 "q-321976" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290139 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534471 true] [17592186290139 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/assets/fbe6faf7f14f9ce51c299f18f9596c08c650079d2ca23398180baff8ea965553.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290139 1113 #uuid "56064967-91d6-5563-9086-6750a198de67" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290139 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the line parallel to the $x$-axis and crossing the center of the circle. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/assets/28d5eaee4de3a6100a92fa017b71d9860ea19cd7073284b41511dda4155ecb6d.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},2\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290140 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290141 13194139534471 true] [17592186290140 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534471 true] [17592186290140 1041 "field-1" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290140 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290141 13194139534471 true] [17592186290140 1110 #uuid "4e4b4b7a-cf4f-5796-81f5-122c37c50baf" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290141 1034 "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290141 1111 #uuid "cd49fd56-01ac-5854-a902-f550deb76254" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290142 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290143 13194139534471 true] [17592186290142 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534471 true] [17592186290142 1041 "field-2" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290142 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290143 13194139534471 true] [17592186290142 1110 #uuid "71e6e4c9-1d12-59dd-9ccf-61b8bd94df92" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290143 1034 "2\\pi" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290143 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290143 1111 #uuid "97704e4c-4b68-5fce-a74c-e5176b1565dc" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290144 1017 #edb/ref 17592186290145 13194139534471 true] [17592186290144 1089 "q-79189" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290144 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534471 true] [17592186290144 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/assets/20c1f81e9737856b7cf1fbf031fd42988a37eb418303114d95f6521c0401fa61.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(4,-3)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-3)?$" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290144 1113 #uuid "fb0431ca-4c38-512f-95e6-f20be49c92fe" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290144 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,-3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290146 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290147 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290148 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290149 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290150 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1041 "selection" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290149 13194139534471 true] [17592186290145 1110 #uuid "71bf6292-beef-5538-8eb0-2e529cd918c6" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290146 1034 "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290146 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290146 1111 #uuid "a78dfa1a-1721-594e-b81c-eb0b5f867298" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290147 1034 "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290147 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290147 1111 #uuid "18f560f8-95b8-59bc-beb4-91ec0024da07" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290148 1034 "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290148 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290148 1111 #uuid "4597e697-7268-536b-a2cb-b32fc6515fc9" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290149 1034 "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290149 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290149 1111 #uuid "b85ea25c-b904-580c-9fed-4d421f9e6f20" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290150 1034 "x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290150 1111 #uuid "dcec6612-2634-5549-a388-e830a9db959b" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290151 1017 #edb/ref 17592186290152 13194139534471 true] [17592186290151 1089 "q-79219" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290151 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534471 true] [17592186290151 1100 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290151 1113 #uuid "e6e752a8-4aef-579f-9278-264f0940936a" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290151 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =49({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =49(1) \\\\  & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49.$" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290153 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290154 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290155 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290156 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290157 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1041 "selection" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290153 13194139534471 true] [17592186290152 1110 #uuid "7cb2aa6a-0626-55d2-858d-6dfbbc9a76a2" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290153 1034 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=49" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290153 1111 #uuid "c5818801-2c7c-51d0-a372-437d3183191b" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290154 1034 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=7" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290154 1111 #uuid "65608b42-0eba-52e4-aa7f-791f6c9d45dc" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290155 1034 "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=56" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290155 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290155 1111 #uuid "3728ebc1-1169-53d4-b35c-621ffd7163b4" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290156 1034 "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=14" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290156 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290156 1111 #uuid "654c2dc3-b68a-506f-979a-6b0a4b943624" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290157 1034 "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{7}" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290157 1111 #uuid "c1fcc665-baa5-594b-bc38-8fac8e7951bf" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290158 1017 #edb/ref 17592186290159 13194139534471 true] [17592186290158 1089 "q-79220" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534471 true] [17592186290158 1100 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290158 1113 #uuid "a4b8a5d2-411d-57b5-b980-2faf8acfc1d1" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290158 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=4.$" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290160 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290161 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290162 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290163 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186290164 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1041 "selection" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1104 #edb/ref 17592186290160 13194139534471 true] [17592186290159 1110 #uuid "f701f652-0721-5be4-b209-ad7612d0d185" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290160 1034 "(x+1)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=4" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290160 1111 #uuid "2cc90685-7f1d-5feb-bdf4-62f0118d2699" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290161 1034 "(x-1)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=2" 13194139534471 true] [17592186290161 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534471 true] [17592186290161 1111 #uuid "5b4219d3-5f90-5660-abc3-8212501f15fd" 13194139534471 t